WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.380
Environment variables are common in shell workflows because they are easy to set, easy to read, and widely supported by CI/CD platforms, containers, and command-line tools.

2
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.760
They can be an appropriate delivery channel when used carefully.

3
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.780
They are not automatically safe.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.880
The fact that a value is not visible in the script file does not mean it cannot appear somewhere else.

5
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.560
The first issue is inheritance.

6
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.420
A process can pass environment variables to child processes.

7
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:40.160
If a script sets a secret in the environment and then launches unrelated tools, wrappers, diagnostics, or helper scripts, those child processes may receive the value too.

8
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.660
Some tools record their environment for debugging or failure analysis.

9
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.640
Some jobs run with verbose traces that reveal expanded commands or configuration.

10
00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.900
Some support bundles collect more context than the original author expected.

11
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.140
Command-line arguments can create similar exposure.

12
00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:03.400
They may be visible in process metadata, diagnostic snapshots, job output, or operator tooling.

13
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:12.780
The safer pattern is to avoid putting sensitive values in broad command lines and to prefer approved mechanisms that keep secrets scoped to the intended process.

14
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:23.040
When a tool supports a protected file, standard input, a platform identity, or a secret reference, that may be preferable to exposing the value as a plain argument.

15
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.760
Defensive scripts treat logs as long-lived records.

16
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:32.780
Logs are read by operators, developers, auditors, vendors, and support staff.

17
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.480
They may be copied into tickets or retained for months.

18
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:45.300
Avoid full environment dumps, avoid printing headers or connection strings, and be cautious with debugging modes that echo commands.

19
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:52.840
If a child process does not need a secret, minimize or scrub the inherited environment before launching it.

20
00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:57.180
This reduces accidental spread without making the workflow harder to understand.

21
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.800
A minimized environment reduces accidental spread without making the workflow harder to understand.
